{% set title = "Methods | " ~ site.name %}
{% set description = "How each reading is made, from the dock to the band call: collection, Coliscan Easygel analysis, the criteria, and the limits of a one-station field method." %}

{% block main %}<div class="grid">
<div class="col-main">
<span class="kicker">Station {{ station.id }}</span>
<h1>Methods</h1>
<p class="lede">How each number is made, from the dock to the band call &mdash; including the parts that are still rough. Anything this page cannot honestly claim yet, it says so.</p>

<h2 id="site">Site</h2>
<p>One fixed station, {{ station.id }}, the Nereid Boat Club dock in Rutherford, New Jersey, on the tidal Lower Passaic below the Dundee Dam. The published coordinate is rounded to two decimal places on purpose: one minor stands in the same place at a regular hour every week.</p>

<h2 id="collection">Collection</h2>
<p>Grab sample from the dock. A sterile bottle is opened about 30 cm below the surface with the mouth facing into the current and the arm extended upstream, capped promptly, dried, labeled with the week and the exact clock time, and put on ice. Plating happens at home inside the holding time. The sampler does not enter the water; the real clock time goes on the sheet, whatever it was.</p>
<p>Sampling began 23 August 2026. The regular window is {{ station.sampling_window }}; the exact time of every sample is a field in the published record, not a promise.</p>

<h2 id="analysis">Analysis</h2>
<p>Coliscan Easygel: 5 mL of sample per plate, poured in duplicate, incubated 24 hours at 35 C. Purple and dark blue colonies are counted as E. coli. Pink, red and magenta colonies are other coliforms &mdash; recorded, not counted as E. coli. Teal, blue-green, white and tan colonies are not counted. The reported value is the mean of the two duplicate counts divided by the plated volume, times 100, giving CFU per 100 mL. At the standard 5 mL that is a multiplier of 20.</p>
<p>Weeks 1 and 2 were plated with more than the nominal 5 mL: the transfer-pipette draws were overfilled, and a later calibration against a marked test tube measured the real volume at about 8.3 mL per plate. Those two records use the measured volume (a multiplier of 12), and each carries the full correction in its comments. From week 3 on, draws stop at the pipette's graduation and the volume is nominal. The correction is documented rather than hidden because the dataset is only worth publishing if it says what actually happened.</p>
<p>Uncountable plates are recorded as uncountable, never estimated. Plates are photographed beside their week label before disposal, and disposal is a 20 minute bleach soak in a sealed bag.</p>

<h2 id="parameters">Field parameters</h2>
<p>Recorded on the dock with each sample: water temperature, pH by field strip, turbidity as depth of visibility in a transparency tube (reported in cm; higher is clearer), nitrate as N and orthophosphate by field test strips, and tide stage.</p>
<p>Antecedent rainfall, the 48 hour total before the sample, is the input the advisory most wants. A project rain gauge at the boathouse is planned; until it is mounted, the rain figure is taken from the nearest airport weather station and each record names that source explicitly. No rain number is ever attributed to a gauge that did not produce it.</p>

<h2 id="criteria">The criteria, and which one each call uses</h2>
<p>Readings are scored against the EPA recreational water quality values for E. coli in fresh water: the 126 CFU/100 mL geometric mean criterion and the 235 CFU/100 mL single sample value. Each week's band is a single sample call: under {{ criteria.geometric_mean }} is green, {{ criteria.geometric_mean }} to {{ criteria.single_sample }} is yellow, over {{ criteria.single_sample }} is red. A season geometric mean will be reported once there are enough weeks for one to mean something; one or two samples are not a geometric mean and this site does not pretend otherwise.</p>

<h2 id="qc">Quality control</h2>
<p>Duplicate plates every week, both counts published. Split samples to an NJDEP certified laboratory for Colilert enumeration are planned for later in the season; none has shipped yet, and until one does, the field method has no external check &mdash; that is the largest known weakness of the current record.</p>

<h2 id="limits">What this cannot tell you</h2>
<p>One station, one time of day, one day a week, a field method rather than a laboratory method, a short record so far, and rainfall currently read from an airport three miles away. This is not a characterization of the Passaic River. It is a characterization of the water at one dock at one hour on the days it was sampled, published with every number needed to check it.</p>
